// Formative practice quiz for sorting-with-comparator. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: collections-streams/collections-in-depth/sorting-with-comparator

::sorting-with-comparator o1 - lowest first by default::Books are sorted with books.sort(Comparator.comparing(Book\:\:pages)). Which book comes first?{
=The book with the fewest pages#Correct. comparing sorts its key in ascending order, smallest first.
~The book with the most pages#Not quite. That needs reversed().
~The book that was added first#Not quite. The sort reorders by pages; only ties keep their previous order.
~The book whose title comes first alphabetically#Not quite. The key is pages, not the title.
}

::sorting-with-comparator o1 - breaking ties::Employees must be sorted by department A to Z, and within a department by salary, highest first. Which comparator does that?{
=comparing(Employee\:\:dept).thenComparing(Comparator.comparing(Employee\:\:salary).reversed())#Correct. The reversal applies only to the salary comparator used as the tie-breaker.
~comparing(Employee\:\:dept).thenComparing(Employee\:\:salary).reversed()#Not quite. reversed() at the end flips the department order too.
~comparing(Employee\:\:salary).reversed().thenComparing(Employee\:\:dept)#Not quite. That makes salary the main key and department only the tie-breaker.
~comparing(Employee\:\:dept).reversed().thenComparing(Employee\:\:salary)#Not quite. That sorts departments Z to A and salaries lowest first.
}

::sorting-with-comparator o1 - ties without a tie-breaker::A list holds cy 80, ben 80 in that order and is sorted with comparing(Player\:\:score). In which order do cy and ben end up?{
=cy, then ben#Correct. List.sort is stable, so elements that compare equal keep their previous order.
~ben, then cy#Not quite. Nothing compares names here; only a tie-breaker would sort them by name.
~In a random order on each run#Not quite. The sort is stable, so the result is predictable.
~The sort throws because of the tie#Not quite. Equal keys are normal; they simply compare as equal.
}

::sorting-with-comparator o2 - natural ordering of strings::What does sorting the list banana, Cherry, apple with sort(null) produce?{
=[Cherry, apple, banana]#Correct. String's natural ordering compares character codes, and uppercase letters come before lowercase.
~[apple, banana, Cherry]#Not quite. That ignores case; natural ordering does not.
~[banana, Cherry, apple]#Not quite. sort(null) does sort, using natural ordering.
~It throws NullPointerException#Not quite. A null comparator means natural ordering.
}

::sorting-with-comparator o2 - where natural ordering comes from::A class Ticket implements Comparable and defines compareTo by ticket number. A support screen must show tickets by priority instead. What is the right approach?{
=Keep compareTo and pass a Comparator built from priority to sort#Correct. A supplied Comparator gives another order without changing the class's natural ordering.
~Change compareTo to compare priority#Not quite. That changes the natural ordering for every other user of Ticket.
~Call sort(null), which sorts by any field you need#Not quite. sort(null) always uses compareTo, here the ticket number.
~Remove Comparable so sort guesses the order#Not quite. Without Comparable, sort(null) throws ClassCastException.
}

::sorting-with-comparator o2 - nulls in the list::A list of strings contains one null. Which call sorts it without an exception and puts the null last?{
=list.sort(Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()))#Correct. nullsLast places null after every non-null element and compares the rest naturally.
~list.sort(null)#Not quite. Natural ordering cannot compare null and throws NullPointerException.
~list.sort(Comparator.naturalOrder())#Not quite. naturalOrder alone also fails on null.
~list.sort(Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder()))#Not quite. That puts the null first.
}
